00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.950
Last time we built the language
of reinforcement learning.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:14.950
States, actions, rewards, and a transition rule
the agent never controls.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.950
From those four pieces we defined the return —
the discounted sum of every reward still to come.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:29.950
That return is the only thing
the agent ever tries to maximize.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.950
We also found that the return
obeys a recursion.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:45.950
The return at time t equals the next reward
plus gamma times the return that follows.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.950
One step now, the whole future
folded into a single discounted term.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:59.950
This recursion is the seed everything
in today's video will grow from.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.950
What we never had in V-01 was a way
to describe how the agent chooses.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:17.950
A policy, written pi of a given s,
is a probability distribution over actions.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.450
It is the probability that the agent
picks action A-sub-t equals a,

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:29.950
when the state is S-sub-t equals s.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:39.950
Notice what this is not — it is not a memorised
solution, not a plan, not an answer.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:48.950
A policy is simply a table of probabilities,
one row per state.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:58.950
Any such table is a valid policy.
Whether it is a good one is a separate question.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:05.950
Here are two policies
for the same environment.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:14.950
On the left, the agent always picks right —
a deterministic policy.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:24.950
On the right, the agent splits its choice
equally across four actions — a stochastic policy.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:33.950
Both are policies.
Neither is more "real" than the other.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:39.950
The deterministic case is just the stochastic case

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:45.950
where one probability is one
and the rest are zero.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:52.950
So policy is not a function
from state to action —

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:59.950
it is a distribution that happens, sometimes,
to put all its mass on one action.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:10.950
Keep this picture in mind.
Today's machinery treats every policy the same way.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:19.950
The randomness we are about to integrate over
lives in this table.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:27.950
Every policy must satisfy
one structural constraint.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:38.950
For each state, the probabilities across actions
must sum to one.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:47.950
That is the only rule.
No other shape is required of pi.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:53.950
For the rest of this video
we adopt the equiprobable policy —

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.950
one quarter, one quarter,
one quarter, one quarter.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:11.950
It is the simplest non-trivial policy we can study,
and it makes the value computations transparent.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:21.950
Every number you are about to see
is measured under this single policy.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:29.950
Change pi, and every number
changes with it.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.950
Now the question
this entire video is built around.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:45.950
How good is state s,
if from here on we follow the policy pi?

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:56.950
We quantify that with the state-value function,
v-sub-pi of s.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:03.450
It is the expectation, under pi,
of the return G-sub-t,

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:09.950
given that we are in state s.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:20.950
Read it slowly: average future return,
starting from s, behaving according to pi.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:29.950
One number per state.
A complete answer to "how good is here?"

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:39.950
Run that definition on every cell of FrozenLake
and you get a grid of numbers.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:49.950
The brighter the cell, the higher the expected
return when the agent starts there.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:06:01.950
Notice the bright corridor leading toward the goal —
that is where the expected return concentrates.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:08.450
The start state sits at 0.012 —
not zero, but small.

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:13.950
The agent is far from the goal
and the ice is slippery.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:20.450
State 14, the cell adjacent to the goal,
reaches 0.434 —

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:26.950
the highest non-terminal value on the board.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:33.450
This grid is v-sub-pi.
The rest of the video is one question:

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:39.950
where did these numbers come from?

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:49.950
Segment 5 will derive them from first principles.
For now, just feel their shape.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:57.950
Look at state 14 —
the cell sitting one step from the goal.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:06.950
FrozenLake gives the agent
zero immediate reward for being there.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:16.950
Zero. There is no bonus for proximity,
no partial credit, no shaping signal.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:26.950
And yet v-sub-pi at state 14 is 0.434 —
the brightest non-terminal cell.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:35.950
That gap — zero immediate reward,
non-zero value — is the entire idea.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:39.950
Value is not what you get now.
Value is what you can expect later.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:45.950
Now a fact that catches
almost everyone the first time.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:54.950
The goal state — the place we want to reach —
has value zero.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:03.950
Not infinity, not a hundred,
not the largest number on the board. Zero.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:12.950
The intuition "the goal should have the highest
value" is wrong, and here is why.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:18.450
Once the agent is at the goal,
the episode is over.

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:24.950
There is no future return left to collect.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:29.950
v-sub-pi of a terminal state is zero by definition —
for the goal and every hole.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:36.950
The state-value answers
"how good is here?"

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:47.950
The action-value function, q-sub-pi of s and a,
answers a slightly different question.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:54.450
How good is it to commit
to action a right now,

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:59.950
then follow pi from the next state onward?

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:09.950
One action of commitment, then back to the policy.
That is the only difference.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:18.950
At state 14, there are four action-values —
one per action.

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:25.450
Going down gives the highest expected return —
0.533 —

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:30.950
because the goal is south.

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:37.450
Going right is almost as good — 0.522 —

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:41.950
the slip can still carry the agent home.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:54.950
Up and left are worse, but never zero.
Even a bad action keeps some hope alive on slippery ice.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:58.950
These four bars are not separate from v-sub-pi.
They are its decomposition.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:09.950
Under the equiprobable policy, v-sub-pi of s
is the average of the four q-values at s.

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:21.950
At state 14, the four q-values average to 0.434 —
exactly the heatmap cell.

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:29.950
In general, v-sub-pi is the policy-weighted sum
of q-sub-pi over actions.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:38.950
Look at what happens when the agent is at state 6,
one step into its future.

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:48.950
First, pi picks an action —
four possible branches, weighted by the policy.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:54.450
Then, for each action,
the environment picks a next state —

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:59.950
three more branches, weighted by p.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:08.950
Two layers of randomness.
Two distinct sums are about to appear in the equation.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:17.950
We are going to derive the Bellman equation,
one identity at a time.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:26.950
We start from the definition
you have already seen.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:33.450
v-sub-pi of s is the expectation, under pi,

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:39.950
of the return given S-sub-t equals s.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:58.950
Nothing new yet. Just the entry point.
Watch what unfolds from here.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:05.950
Recall the recursion from V-01.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:17.950
G-sub-t equals R-sub-t-plus-1
plus gamma times G-sub-t-plus-1.

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:29.950
Substitute that inside the expectation,
and the return splits into two pieces.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:41.950
The next reward, plus the discounted
future return from the next state.

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:48.450
The expectation now ranges
over both pieces,

00:12:48.500 --> 00:12:54.950
because both depend on the policy
and the environment.

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:59.950
We pull the randomness out, one source at a time.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:06.950
First, pi —
the agent's choice of action.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:13.450
Replace expectation-under-pi
with an outer sum over actions,

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:19.950
weighted by pi of a given s.

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:31.950
That is the policy contribution to the expectation,
made explicit.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:38.950
Notice on the diagram — the four action branches
now glow in sync with the new sum.

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:49.950
Inside each action branch lies the second source
of randomness — the environment.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:14:00.950
Replace what remains with an inner sum
over next-state and reward pairs.

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:12.950
Each pair is weighted by
p of s-prime and r given s and a.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:24.950
The outer sum is over actions.
The inner sum is over transitions. They never collapse into one.

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:28.950
Two sums, two sources of randomness —
exactly as the backup diagram promised.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:35.950
One last move,
and the recursion closes.

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:48.950
The expected return from the next state is,
by definition, v-sub-pi of that next state.

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:59.950
Substitute, and v-sub-pi appears
on both sides of the equation.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:10.950
Outside the sum, evaluated at s.
Inside the sum, evaluated at s-prime.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:18.950
v-sub-pi is the fixed point of this relationship —
the function that is consistent with itself under pi.

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:29.950
This is the Bellman expectation equation
for v-sub-pi.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:36.450
Outer sum over actions,
weighted by the policy.

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:42.950
Inner sum over transitions,
weighted by p.

00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:53.950
It is the single most important relationship
in everything that follows in this course.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:58.950
Let it sit for a moment.

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:06.950
The Bellman equation is an equality,
not an instruction.

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:15.950
The true v-sub-pi satisfies it
at every state, simultaneously.

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:26.950
Take state 6. The heatmap reports
v-sub-pi of 6 is approximately 0.039.

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:38.950
Now evaluate the right-hand side
from the converged values — the same 0.039 falls out.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:49.950
Left side and right side agree at machine precision.
The identity holds.

00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:58.950
The Bellman equation does not compute v-sub-pi.
It checks that v-sub-pi is itself.

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:06.950
The equation can be read as math,
or it can be read as code.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:17.950
On the right is the exact same relationship,
written in Python against the Gymnasium API.

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:29.950
One outer loop over actions.
One inner loop over the transitions returned by env-dot-P.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:38.950
Every symbol in the equation
has a variable in the code waiting to receive it.

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:46.950
Watch how three views move together.

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:57.950
pi of state and action is pi of a given s —
the policy weight for the outer sum.

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:10.950
prob is p of s-prime and r given s and a —
the transition weight for the inner sum.

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:22.950
reward is r — the immediate reward
returned by the environment on that transition.

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:30.450
And gamma times v-prev of next-state
is gamma times v-sub-pi of s-prime —

00:18:30.500 --> 00:18:37.950
the discounted value
of where the agent lands.

00:18:38.000 --> 00:18:52.950
Each line of Python is one term of the Bellman
equation, applied to one cell of the grid.

00:18:53.000 --> 00:19:07.950
The equation, the code, and the geometry
are three views of a single object.

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:28.950
Trust the link. From here on, when we write code,
we are writing the Bellman equation.

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:37.950
Run that code once,
starting from a guess of all zeros.

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:47.950
The right-hand side at state 6
evaluates to 0.000000.

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:54.450
One sweep is not enough —
the heatmap takes many sweeps to emerge.

00:19:54.500 --> 00:19:58.950
V-03 is where we iterate this loop
until the numbers stop changing.

00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:07.950
Back to the equation
that organizes everything we have done.

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:21.950
Outer sum, policy. Inner sum, environment.
Recursion, closure. Identity, not algorithm.

00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:28.950
One picture to carry forward.

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:39.950
What if you do not know v-sub-pi?
What if you only have a guess?

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:50.950
Turn the equality into an assignment.
Sweep every state. Repeat until nothing changes.

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:58.950
That algorithm is the next video — policy evaluation.
v-sub-pi is the fixed point it converges to.

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:10.950
A policy is a distribution over actions.
A state-value function is the expected return under that policy.

00:21:11.000 --> 00:21:21.950
The Bellman equation is the self-consistency
the true state-value function must satisfy.

00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:29.433
Next time we turn that consistency
into a computation.
